<h2>How the Print DPI / Resolution Calculator works</h2><p>For raster artwork, required pixels equal physical document inches × required pixels per inch. The tool adds bleed to both sides of the trim dimensions, calculates the pixel dimensions needed at the entered PPI, and compares them with the current artwork. Effective PPI is based on the limiting width or height.</p><h2>How to use this print dpi / resolution calculator</h2><p>Enter final trim size, bleed per edge and the image-resolution requirement supplied by the printer. Then enter the raster artwork’s pixel dimensions. A 300 PPI target is common in many commercial-print workflows, but Adobe notes that required image resolution depends on the output process, printer resolution and screen frequency, so the provider’s specification should be used.</p><h2>How to interpret the result</h2><p>A “meets target” result means the pixel dimensions are at least as large as the calculated requirement for the full bleed document. It does not mean the file is otherwise press-ready. Color space, embedded profiles, transparency, overprint, fonts, black generation and PDF standard are separate production checks.</p><h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2><p>Image PPI is often incorrectly called DPI. Printer DPI describes output-device dots; image resolution is pixels per inch. Vector artwork does not need a fixed pixel count until rasterized, although embedded raster images do. Cropping or scaling an image in layout software changes its effective PPI.</p><h2>Practical example and workflow</h2><p>An 8.5 × 11 inch piece with 0.125 inch bleed becomes 8.75 × 11.25 inches. At 300 PPI, a full-page raster image needs at least 2625 × 3375 pixels. This calculator lets prepress check that requirement before creating the final PDF.</p>
